---
/**
 * TweetCard — a saved x.com post, as X renders it, with a way back to the
 * notes underneath.
 *
 * **This shipped as a static card first and Aayush reversed it.** The card was
 * react-tweet's anatomy rebuilt from files in this repository — a monogram in
 * place of the avatar, the words, a media grid, no script, no request. Every
 * argument for it still holds and it still loads nothing; what it could not do
 * was look right. His review of the shipped wall: the cards look kind of odd.
 * A hand-built imitation of a tweet is uncanny in the way an imitation of
 * anything familiar is, and no amount of matching their CSS closes that,
 * because what is missing is the avatar and the marks a copy cannot fetch.
 *
 * So the card is now the official embed, and the thing that used to be the
 * whole card is the **fallback inside the blockquote**: what a reader sees
 * before X's factory arrives, what they see for ever with scripting off, and
 * the height the masonry column reserves in the meantime.
 * `components/XEmbeds.astro` carries what that costs and who decided it.
 *
 * ## Two anchors now, and that is the rule changing rather than breaking
 *
 * The old card was one link on purpose: a press anywhere on it went to one
 * place, so it could never be a coin toss. An embed cannot keep that — every
 * link inside X's iframe goes to X, and none of them is ours to redirect. So
 * the destination this site cares about moves **outside** the embed, into a bar
 * under it that says `notes` and goes to `/library/<slug>`.
 *
 * That is the video tile's arrangement rather than a new one: two regions with
 * two obvious affordances, press the thing and you get the thing, press the
 * label and you get the page about it. What the card must never be is one
 * region with two meanings, and it is not.
 *
 * The permalink at the foot of the fallback is the third anchor and it is only
 * ever seen when the embed has not loaded. It is what X's factory reads the
 * post's id out of, and it wears the `.ext` arrow for the reason the row's
 * `source` link does: it is the one control here that ends the visit.
 *
 * ## What the fallback still refuses
 *
 * No avatar, no brand mark, no `pbs.twimg.com`: the pictures are files in this
 * repository and `lib/library.ts › readShotPath` makes that structural rather
 * than careful — the parser will not carry a remote URL, so no fallback can
 * render one. The monogram is still the avatar slot with a letter in it. All of
 * that is about what *this site* fetches before X's factory runs, and it is
 * still nothing.
 *
 * The text is one paragraph because that is the shape it arrives in: the
 * pipeline flattens a post's markdown, so `Post.text` carries no newline to
 * preserve. It is cut at `POST_CARD_MAX` for the reason `lib/post.ts` gives at
 * length, which now applies only to the fallback — X's own embed decides where
 * to cut a long post inside its frame.
 */
import type { LibraryEntry, Post } from "../lib/library";
import { entryHref } from "../lib/library";
import { hueSlot } from "../lib/tags";
import { POST_CARD_MAX, clipText, monogram } from "../lib/post";

interface Props {
  /** The entry the card links to. Its `post` is what the card draws. */
  entry: LibraryEntry & { post: Post };
}

const { entry } = Astro.props;
const { post } = entry;

/** Where a /library thing sends a reader, shared with the row and the tile. */
const href = entryHref(entry);

const initial = monogram(post.author);
const body = clipText(post.text, POST_CARD_MAX);
---

<li class="card">
  {/*
    `twitter-tweet` is what X's factory looks for and `data-dnt` is the one
    thing we can ask it for: do not use this visit to tailor anything. The
    theme arrives from `XEmbeds.astro` before the factory does, because an
    iframe on another origin cannot be re-themed once it exists.

    Everything inside is the fallback. It is real content rather than a
    placeholder — with scripting off it is the whole card, and until the
    factory lands it is what holds the column open.
  */}
  <blockquote class="card__quote twitter-tweet" data-dnt="true">
    <span class="card__head">
      {
        /*
         * Hidden from the accessibility tree: the letter is a recognition aid
         * for the name sitting beside it, and announcing "B" before "Ben Lang"
         * is a stutter, not a fact. Same call `XGlyph.astro` makes.
         *
         * Absent entirely when the display name has no letter or digit in it,
         * because an empty coloured circle claims an identity it is not
         * carrying (`lib/post.ts › monogram`).
         */
        initial !== "" && (
          <span class="monogram" data-hue={hueSlot(post.handle)} aria-hidden="true">
            {initial}
          </span>
        )
      }

      <span class="card__who">
        <span class="card__author">{post.author}</span>
        <span class="card__meta mono">
          <span class="card__handle">@{post.handle}</span>
          <span class="card__date tabular-nums">{post.date}</span>
        </span>
      </span>
    </span>

    <p class="card__text">{body}</p>

    {
      post.media.length > 0 && (
        <span class="card__media" data-count={post.media.length}>
          {post.media.map((src, index) => (
            <img
              class="outlined"
              src={src}
              alt={
                post.media.length === 1
                  ? `Picture attached to the post by ${post.author}`
                  : `Picture ${index + 1} of ${post.media.length} attached to the post by ${post.author}`
              }
              loading="lazy"
              decoding="async"
            />
          ))}
        </span>
      )
    }

    {/*
      The post itself, and the last anchor in the blockquote on purpose: X's
      factory reads the id out of it. `source` is the word the /library row
      uses for the same control, so a reader who has seen one has seen both,
      and the title rides along where only a screen reader hears it.
    */}
    <a
      class="card__source mono ext"
      href={entry.url}
      rel="noopener nofollow"
      target="_blank"
    >
      source<span class="visually-hidden">: the post by {post.author}</span>
    </a>
  </blockquote>

  {/*
    Outside the embed, because everything inside one belongs to X. This is the
    card's own destination and the only one on it that stays here.
  */}
  <p class="card__notes">
    <a class="card__link mono press" href={href}>
      notes<span class="visually-hidden"> on {entry.title}</span>
    </a>
  </p>
</li>

<style>
  /* The item in the wall. `PostWall.astro` owns the columns; what belongs to a
     card is that it must not be split across one, and the air under it. */
  .card {
    break-inside: avoid;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

  /* The fallback, wearing the card the static version was.

     X hides this element and puts its own iframe beside it once the factory
     runs, so everything below is what a reader sees before that and instead of
     it. The border is the site's hairline and the corners are sharp, which is
     design.md §3: a radius marks a token you could pick up, never a surface you
     look through. X's own frame is rounded and that is theirs. */
  .card__quote {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    /* The height the masonry column holds while the factory is in flight.
       X's shortest rendered card is a little under this, so a very short post
       reserves roughly what it is about to become instead of collapsing to
       four lines and then jumping. Every post saved here is taller than it
       already, so it costs the current data nothing. */
    min-height: 8rem;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 1rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--hairline);
    border-radius: 0;
  }

  /* The fallback stands down once the embed is there, and we do it rather than
     waiting for X to.

     Their factory inserts the rendered widget as a sibling and marks the
     blockquote it consumed with a `data-twitter-extracted-<session id>`
     attribute — an attribute name nothing can select on, because the id changes
     per page load — and, measured on the shipped page, leaves it visible. Two
     copies of the same post, one ours and one theirs, stacked.

     So the swap is keyed on `.twitter-tweet-rendered`, the class their widget
     has carried for years and the one every site styling an embed already
     depends on, plus the fact that they insert it immediately before the
     blockquote they took it from. Two assumptions about somebody else's markup,
     and both fail the same harmless way: the fallback stays visible under the
     embed. Visible and wrong beats silent and wrong for a dependency like this.

     `:global` sits in a plain descendant position and deliberately not inside
     a `:has()`. Astro rewrites the scoped halves of a selector and leaves
     `:global(...)` in place when it is nested in a functional pseudo-class, so
     the browser gets a selector it cannot parse and drops the whole rule.
     **That shipped for one build here**, silently, which is the same failure
     mode this comment is about. `VideoFacade.astro › .tile__frame
     :global(iframe)` is the shape that works. */
  :global(.twitter-tweet-rendered) + .card__quote {
    display: none;
  }

  .card__head {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.65rem;
    min-width: 0;
  }

  .card__who {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.2rem;
    min-width: 0;
  }

  .card__author {
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    line-height: 1.3;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .card__meta {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0 0.4rem;
    color: var(--faint);
  }

  /* Drawn, never typed into the content: design.md §6. Keyed to the item after
     it, the same way the footer's colophon does it, so a wrapped line starts
     with the separator instead of the line above ending on a dangling one. The
     `margin-right` matches the flex gap on its other side, and the colour is
     the one every separator on the site takes. */
  .card__handle + .card__date::before {
    content: "\00b7" / "";
    margin-right: 0.4rem;
    color: var(--hairline-strong);
  }

  .card__date {
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .card__text {
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--muted);
    /* A post arrives flattened to one paragraph, so nothing here has to survive
       a newline. `overflow-wrap` is for the other thing a post is full of: a
       t.co shortlink with no word boundary in it, which would otherwise run out
       past the card's own border. */
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* The quiet pattern, the same one `LibraryList.astro › .row__source` takes:
     `--muted` because it is a control and design.md §4 is the floor, and its
     own underline because on touch there is no hover to reveal one. */
  .card__source {
    align-self: flex-start;
    color: var(--muted);
    text-decoration-line: underline;
    text-decoration-color: var(--hairline-strong);
    transition-property: color, text-decoration-color;
    transition-duration: var(--dur-fast);
    transition-timing-function: var(--ease);
  }

  .card__source:hover {
    color: var(--accent);
    text-decoration-color: currentColor;
  }

  /* The way back to this site, and the one thing on the card that survives
     hydration. A box of its own rather than a rule under the embed: after the
     swap it is sitting under an iframe with its own rounded border, and a bare
     word there reads as something that fell off the card above it.

     40px on the nose, which is design.md §4's floor taken literally rather than
     through an `::before`. The stated text exception is for a word in a row of
     words; this is the only affordance on the card that stays on the site, on a
     page that is a wall of other people's frames, and a control that has to be
     obvious does not get to be a hint. It is also inside the 46px the thebridge
     reference sets its own list rows at. */
  .card__notes {
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--hairline);
    /* The anchor inside carries `.press`, which already names `border-color`
       and `color` — but the border is on this box and not on it, so the box
       needs its own. One property, named, never `all` (design.md §4). */
    transition-property: border-color;
    transition-duration: var(--dur-fast);
    transition-timing-function: var(--ease);
  }

  .card__link {
    /* A card-width target; 0.96 would visibly shove it sideways. The number the
       card itself used to carry, and `SiteGrid.astro › .card__link` before it,
       because this is the same kind of thing at the same kind of width. */
    --press-scale: 0.985;

    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.4rem;
    min-height: 40px;
    padding-inline: 1rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  /* Drawn rather than typed, and the alt-text form so it has no accessible
     name of its own: the link is already called `notes on <title>`, and an
     arrow read out after it is furniture being announced. Not the `.ext` arrow,
     which means the visit ends — this one goes one page further in. */
  .card__link::after {
    content: "\2192" / "";
  }

  /* The link fills the box, so hovering one is hovering the other: the border
     is keyed to the box and the ink to the anchor, and nothing needs `:has()`
     to say so. Not gated behind `@media (hover: hover)` — pressing this unloads
     the page, so the touch latch design.md §4 gates for is gone before it could
     be seen. */
  .card__notes:hover {
    border-color: var(--hairline-strong);
  }

  .card__link:hover {
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  /* The media grid, react-tweet's proportions.

     2px is their gap and it is the right number: any more and four pictures
     read as four pictures rather than as one attachment. The box is reserved by
     `aspect-ratio` rather than by width/height attributes, because nothing here
     knows a picture's size at build time (design.md §8).

     **8:5 is not a new number.** It is what `ShotFrame.astro › .preview__shot`
     already crops an unknown-sized picture to on a card, and a second answer to
     one question is how a site ends up with two crops that nearly match. It
     also happens to be the ratio that makes a 2x2 read as four landscape cells
     rather than four slots.

     Four is as many pictures as x.com lets a post carry, so 1, 2, 3 and 4 are
     the whole vocabulary. A fifth would take a third row and squeeze all of
     them inside the reserved box, which is the honest failure: it shows
     everything, badly, instead of hiding one. */
  .card__media {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    grid-auto-rows: 1fr;
    gap: 2px;
    aspect-ratio: 8 / 5;
    /* The fallback is a column flex container, so this box's automatic minimum
       height is its content — which is the reserved box's own contents, so the
       ratio above would compute a height and then be overruled by it. Zeroing
       the minimum is what lets `aspect-ratio` be the answer. The `min-height`
       on the pictures below is the same rule one level in, for the rows. */
    min-height: 0;
  }

  .card__media[data-count="1"] {
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
  }

  /* The L: the first picture holds the left column at full height and the other
     two stack beside it. One rule, because the grid places the rest itself. */
  .card__media[data-count="3"] > :first-child {
    grid-row: span 2;
  }

  .card__media img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    /* A grid item's automatic minimum size is its content, so a portrait
       screenshot in a `1fr` row pushes the row past the reserved box and the
       `aspect-ratio` above quietly stops meaning anything. **This shipped
       first**: four 1200px-tall shots came out 484px deep in a box asked for
       208. Zeroing both minimums is what lets `1fr` be a fraction. */
    min-width: 0;
    min-height: 0;
    object-fit: cover;
    /* Anchored to the top, the same call and the same reason as
       `ShotFrame.astro › .preview__shot`: what gets attached to a post is
       usually a screenshot or a chart, and the top of one is the half that
       says what it is. */
    object-position: top center;
    /* Sharp, like every other frame on the site. */
    border-radius: 0;
  }
</style>
